.Dd October 2, 2026
.Dt SLENDMAIL 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm slendmail ,
.Nm sendmail ,
.Nm newaliases ,
.Nm mailq
.Nd deliver the mail of system tools to chat and webhook services
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl tiIv
.Op Fl f Ar sender
.Op Fl F Ar name
.Op Fl o Ar option
.Op Fl b Ar mode
.Op Fl q Ns Op Ar interval
.Op Fl -config Ar path
.Op Fl -
.Op Ar recipient ...
.Nm
.Fl -version | -help | -status
.Nm newaliases
.Nm mailq
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads one message on standard input and delivers it to the targets of
.Pa /etc/slendmail.conf .
It is installed as
.Pa /usr/sbin/sendmail
and accepts the command lines of cron, at, sudo, mdadm, mailx and similar
callers.
.Pp
Flags follow
.Xr getopt 3 :
a value is glued
.Pq Fl froot
or the next argument
.Pq Fl f Ar root ,
flags without a value group
.Pq Fl ti ,
and
.Fl -
ends the options.
Options and recipients may come in any order before
.Fl - .
The value of a flag is never read as an option.
An unknown flag is logged and ignored.
.Pp
Recipients are the arguments, split at commas, and with
.Fl t
the addresses of the To, Cc and Bcc headers, without duplicates.
A message with a Resent-To, Resent-Cc or Resent-Bcc header, even an empty one,
is a resent message: then
.Fl t
takes the addresses of these three headers instead.
Bcc and Resent-Bcc headers are removed from the message.
.Pa /etc/aliases
and
.Pa .forward
are not read.
.Pp
The envelope sender is the value of
.Fl f
or
.Fl r ,
else the first Resent-From address, else the From address, else
.Ev EMAIL ,
else
.Ev USER
or
.Ev LOGNAME
at the host name, else the login name of the real user id at the host name.
When the setgid bit applies, the environment is skipped; a user id without an
entry in the user database gives an empty sender.
.Pp
The text of a notification is the text/plain part of the message, else its
text/html part converted to text; other parts are attachments.
The conversion collapses white space outside pre elements and reduces several
empty lines in a row to one everywhere, pre elements included.
Encoded words, quoted-printable and base64 are decoded and every charset is
converted to UTF-8.
A body declared US-ASCII, or in an unknown charset, that is valid UTF-8 is
taken as UTF-8.
Text without a charset or declared US-ASCII that is not valid UTF-8, and text
declared ISO-8859-1, is read as windows-1252.
.Pp
The message goes to all targets at the same time, each in the markup of its
service, or as it is to a program.
A text longer than a target accepts is cut, counted the way the service counts
it: a subject longer than a quarter of the limit is cut at a word and ends
with
.Dq ... ,
then the list of attachments and then the body, which ends with a notice.
When the rest of the text alone is too long, the text is cut hard, for
Telegram between tags and entities with the open tags closed.
An attachment the target sends files but not this one, over its size or number
of files, stays in the list with a notice.
.Pp
With the default
.Ic on_long No = Qq file
of a target that sends files, a cut text is followed by the full text as
.Pa message.txt ,
the first file of the message, or with
.Ic long_file No = Qq eml
by the message itself without its Bcc fields as
.Pa message.eml .
.Ic on_long No = Qq truncate ,
or a target without files, ends the cut body with the size of the full text
instead;
.Qq blockquote
is
.Qq file
with the cut body in an expandable blockquote in Telegram.
.Ic max_text
replaces the limit of the service and
.Ic max_lines
cuts the body after that many lines.
A target that rejects the text with 400, as Telegram does with markup it cannot
parse, gets the full text once more as a file alone.
.Pp
.Ic template ,
a Go text/template, or
.Ic template_file ,
the absolute path of a file holding one, replaces the built-in template of a
target of any type but
.Cm exec .
It writes the markup of the target, Telegram HTML for
.Cm telegram
and the JSON body for
.Cm http ,
and escapes what comes from the message with
.Ic tgHTML ,
.Ic discordEscape ,
.Ic slackEscape ,
.Ic mmEscape ,
.Ic toJson
and the like.
Besides the functions of text/template it has
.Ic toUpper ,
.Ic toLower ,
.Ic trimSpace ,
.Ic trimEnd ,
.Ic title ,
.Ic default ,
.Ic join ,
.Ic match ,
.Ic reReplaceAll ,
.Ic tz ,
.Ic date ,
.Ic lines ,
.Ic head ,
.Ic tail ,
.Ic truncate ,
.Ic indent
and
.Ic humanizeBytes .
A template that does not parse, or uses define, template or block, is a
configuration error.
One rendering may take 1 s and write 1 MiB.
A template that fails while rendering a message, past either limit, with an
error of a function or with nothing but white space, leaves that message the
built-in template, as does a 400 to its text, once; the log records
.Dq template failed, built-in used .
A template that ran out of time stays failed for the rest of the process.
.Sh TARGETS
Each
.Li "[target.NAME]"
table of the configuration is one target; its
.Ic type
selects the service:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm telegram
Bot API
.Cm sendMessage
in the HTML parse mode, text of at most 4096 characters after entities, an
emoji counted once, and one
.Cm sendDocument
per attachment, up to 10 files of 50 MB; a text of at most 1024 characters
goes as the caption of the first document instead.
.It Cm discord
One webhook execution with wait=true and no mention allowed, text of at most
2000 characters, up to 10 files of 20 MiB and 25 MiB together.
.It Cm slack
Web API
.Cm chat.postMessage
with the bot token, text of at most 40000 characters, and up to 10 files
shared in the thread of the message.
.It Cm ntfy
A JSON publish with the subject as title, text of at most 4096 bytes, and one
more message per attachment, up to 10 files of 15 MiB.
.It Cm http
One POST, or PUT or PATCH with
.Ic method ,
of the JSON document of its preset,
.Cm mattermost ,
.Cm slack-webhook
or
.Cm generic-json ,
or of its
.Ic template ;
extra headers override the Content-Type.
Only
.Cm slack-webhook
is cut, to 40000 characters, unless
.Ic max_text
sets a limit.
.It Cm exec
Runs the program of
.Ic argv ,
an array whose first element is an absolute path, without a shell.
Its stdin is the message without its Bcc and Resent-Bcc fields; its
environment holds only
.Ev PATH ,
.Ev LANG Ns = Ns Li C.UTF-8 ,
.Ev TZ
when set, and
.Ev SLENDMAIL_SUBJECT ,
.Ev SLENDMAIL_FROM ,
.Ev SLENDMAIL_TO
(the recipients without blind copies),
.Ev SLENDMAIL_HOSTNAME ,
.Ev SLENDMAIL_TARGET ,
.Ev SLENDMAIL_MSGID
and
.Ev SLENDMAIL_SIZE ,
each with line breaks and NUL turned into spaces and cut to 4096 bytes.
Exit status 0 is a delivery, 75 a temporary failure, anything else a
permanent one.
Past
.Ic timeout ,
30 s by default, or the deadline, the process group of the program is
killed and the failure is temporary; so it is after SIGINT, SIGTERM or SIGHUP
to
.Nm
once the message is read, while before that such a signal discards the
message.
A setgid call runs the program with the real user and group ids of the
caller; a queue run as the slendmail user runs it with the slendmail group.
.Ic argv
is visible to all local users and holds no secret; a secret the program reads
itself is readable by every caller of a setgid call.
Its stdout and stderr, up to 4096 bytes, are logged.
.It Cm shoutrrr
The plain text through the service of the shoutrrr library that the scheme of
.Ic url
selects, without files; an unknown scheme, a URL the service rejects or a
matrix URL with a user name instead of an access token is a configuration
error.
A failure after an HTTP answer is classified by its status, any other failure
is temporary.
A binary built with the noshoutrrr tag rejects the target as a configuration
error.
.El
.Pp
.Ic max_file_size
replaces the size limit of one file, in bytes, for every type but
.Cm http ,
.Cm exec
and
.Cm shoutrrr ;
a bigger file is listed as not sent.
.Pp
429 and 5xx answers, and any answer with a Retry-After header, are temporary
failures; other rejections are permanent.
A target that took the text but not all files counts as delivered.
.Sh QUEUE
Before the delivery the message is written to the spool,
.Pa /var/spool/slendmail
unless
.Ic dir
of the
.Li "[spool]"
table names another directory or is empty, which turns the spool off.
The entry keeps the raw message and the state of each target; it is removed
once every target has the message or has rejected it.
A target that fails temporarily is tried again by a later queue run, one
minute after the first failure, twice as long after each further failure, at
most after 24 hours and never before the Retry-After of the service; later
entries skip a target that asked for a delay until it ends.
An entry older than 7 days moves to
.Pa failed/ ,
which keeps it 30 days; for a message that was held the 7 days count from
its release.
A retry renders the message with the configuration of the moment, to the
targets fixed when it was queued; a target no longer configured fails for it.
Delivery is at least once: a process killed after a service accepted the
message, a timeout after that, or a power failure repeats the delivery.
.Pp
A message whose configuration is rejected is kept in
.Pa hold/ ,
in the default spool when the file cannot be read at all; the next queue run
with a valid configuration sends it to every target, and
.Fl q
moves it from the default spool when the valid file names another
.Ic dir .
.Pp
A call runs the queue briefly after its own message: the caller's entries
only, at most 20 of them within 10 seconds, skipped when another call of the
same user runs it.
.Fl q
runs it for up to 60 seconds: all entries for root, the
.Li slendmail
user and a caller without the setgid bit, the caller's own entries for
everyone else.
An entry another process is working on is skipped.
The packages run
.Fl q
as
.Li slendmail
every 5 minutes, by a systemd timer or by cron.
.Pp
The spool holds at most 1000 messages and 256 MiB, and 200 messages and
64 MiB per user; users other than root and
.Li slendmail
fill at most 80% of the totals.
A message over a limit is delivered without an entry.
The spool must be on a local file system: the locks do not work over NFS.
Missing areas are created in an existing directory; with the setgid bit the
packages create them, owned by root and the
.Li slendmail
group, mode 2770.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl f Ar sender , Fl r Ar sender
Envelope sender, angle brackets around it removed; an empty value or
.Li <>
is the null sender.
Without a value or with a line break the call exits 64.
.It Fl F Ar name
Full name of the sender; a line break exits 64.
.It Fl t
Take recipients from the To, Cc and Bcc headers as well, or only from the
Resent-To, Resent-Cc and Resent-Bcc headers when the message has any of them.
.It Fl i , Fl oi
A line with a single dot is ordinary text.
Without it such a line ends the message, and the first dot of a line
starting with two dots is removed.
.It Fl o Ar option
Ignored, except
.Fl oi .
.It Fl b Ar mode
The first letter decides:
.Cm m
delivers, the default;
.Cm i
does nothing;
.Cm p
lists the queue;
.Cm s
is refused with exit status 64; other modes are ignored with a warning.
.It Fl q Ns Op Ar interval
Runs the queue once, see
.Sx QUEUE ;
the interval is ignored.
.It Fl I
Does nothing, like
.Fl bi .
.It Fl C Ar file
Ignored with a warning.
.It Fl B , Fl h , Fl L , Fl N , Fl O , Fl R , Fl V , Fl X , Fl p , Fl A
Take a value and are ignored.
.It Fl d , Fl e
Ignored; the value is glued or the next argument unless it starts with a
dash.
.It Fl v , Fl m , Fl n , Fl U , Fl G
Ignored.
.It Fl -config Ar path
Read another configuration file; ignored with a warning when the setgid
bit of the binary applies to a caller other than root.
.It Fl -version
Print the version.
.It Fl -help
Print a usage summary.
.It Fl -status
Print the numbers of queued, held and failed messages, the files in
.Pa tmp/ ,
the bytes of the spool and the age of the oldest entry as one logfmt line.
.El
.Pp
Called as
.Nm newaliases
the program does nothing; called as
.Nm mailq ,
or with
.Fl bp ,
it lists the queue: every entry with the state of each target for root, the
.Li slendmail
user and a caller without the setgid bit, the numbers alone for everyone
else; a spool directory that does not exist lists as empty.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev SLENDMAIL_CONFIG
Another configuration file, like
.Fl -config .
.It Ev EMAIL , Ev USER , Ev LOGNAME
Sender of a message without
.Fl f
and without a Resent-From or From header; ignored when the setgid bit
applies.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /etc/slendmail.conf
The configuration.
.It Pa /var/spool/slendmail/queue/ , Pa hold/ , Pa failed/
Entries: the message and its state beside it.
.It Pa /var/spool/slendmail/tmp/
Files being written; a queue run removes those older than one hour.
.It Pa /var/spool/slendmail/locks/
Queue run locks, one per user.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0
At least one target accepted the message, the rest is queued, or a mode
without a message succeeded.
.It 64
Usage error: a sender flag without a value, a line break in an address
or in the full name,
.Fl bs ,
or a mode that does not exist yet.
.It 66
Standard input cannot be read.
.It 69
No target accepted the message: one rejected it, or all failed temporarily
with the spool off.
.It 70
Internal error.
.It 73
A target failed temporarily and the spool entry could not be created: the
directory is missing or not writable, or a limit is reached.
.It 74
A target failed temporarily and the spool entry could not be written,
.Fl q
could not read the spool, or
.Fl -status
found no spool directory.
.It 77
A service mode requested by a caller without the right to it.
.It 78
The configuration cannot be read or is invalid; the message is held.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr syslog 3 ,
.Xr cron 8
